          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Bread primarily signifies Christ himself—especially his body in the Eucharist—and, more broadly, God-given life and sustenance. The Word of God and the purity/corruption contrast involving leaven are recognized secondary meanings.\",\"book_summary\":\"Bread signifies the word or doctrine of God; unleavened bread is pure doctrine, while leavened bread is doctrine corrupted by error.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Bread = Christ, the Bread of Life and sacramental body, as the supreme form of divine spiritual sustenance.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Bread = the word of God or doctrine, qualified as pure or corrupt according to the absence or presence of leaven.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book promotes a recognized secondary interpretation—word or doctrine—to the controlling identification and applies it to both unleavened and leavened bread. It thereby omits or displaces central consensus cases such as Christ himself, his Eucharistic body, ordinary divine provision, and ecclesial fellowship, while reclassifying leaven distinctions as doctrinal purity versus corruption.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"As a primary or general identification, the book replaces the consensus referent of Christ/divine sustenance with a different referent, God's word or doctrine. The claims can coexist as context-dependent interpretations, but they are not equivalent when offered as the governing meaning of bread.\",\"rationale\":\"The entry is not novel because every consensus response recognizes bread as the Word of God or spiritual truth, and several recognize unleavened bread or leaven as a purity-versus-corruption motif. Nevertheless, the consensus consistently treats Christ and his sacramental body as the dominant core, whereas the book identifies bread directly with doctrine and organizes its meaning by leaven. This is replacement of the central referent rather than mere narrowing of the Christological identification.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Deuteronomy 8:3 and Matthew 4:4 contrast ordinary bread with God's word rather than directly equating bread with that word. Matthew 16:12 explicitly associates leaven with doctrine, but not bread itself; 1 Corinthians 5:7-8 supports a figurative contrast between leavened malice and unleavened sincerity and truth. Thus the citations support the leaven-based purity/corruption distinction more directly than the blanket equation of bread with the word of God.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
